{
  "title": "mapdata.dat",
  "description": "mapdata.dat gives the folder of each campaign mission (see Appendix: Missions list). The entry number is the game's mission number: 0 means no mission, 1 is the Terran tutorial and 64 is the last mission of the Brood War Zerg campaign. The file only gives the folders. Which missions make up each campaign, their order, their races and their cinematics are fixed in the executable.\n\nThe file is arr\\mapdata.dat in the game's MPQ archives. It is 260 bytes long: one 4-byte entry for each of the 65 missions.\n\nThe layout is the same in every version from 1.04 to 1.17, in both StarCraft and Brood War. StarCraft 1.00 to 1.03 used an older layout with 32 missions (128 bytes). That older file is still in StarDat.mpq in later versions, but it is never loaded, because the game finds the copy in BrooDat.mpq (or patch_rt.mpq) first.\n\nThe game loads the file once, when the program starts. StarEdit does not use it. If the file is longer than the layout, the game stops with a file error. A shorter file loads without an error if it ends exactly where a field ends; the missing fields then keep whatever they held before. Out-of-range values are not rejected: the game then reads whatever follows the table that the value points into.\n\nThe game loads a mission's map from <folder>\\staredit\\scenario.chk in its MPQ archives. The sounds played by the mission's briefing and triggers are loaded from <folder>\\<the file name in the map>. The game also uses the last part of each folder to find the mission that belongs to a map file of the same name (.scm files among the original missions, other files among the Brood War missions).",
  "spec": [
    {
      "name": "Mission Folder",
      "offset": "0x0000",
      "length": "260 bytes (4 bytes / Mission)",
      "description": "A string number in arr\\mapdata.tbl, counting from 1: 1 is the first string. The game turns it into the string itself when it loads the file. Only the lowest 16 bits are used; 0 means no string, and a number beyond the last string gives an empty string. The string is the mission's folder, for example campaign\\terran\\terran01.",
      "values": "Integer value between 0 and the number of strings in arr\\mapdata.tbl. In the shipped file, entry n holds n + 1.",
      "bits": []
    }
